---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="Privacy policy for yekipo's desktop tools, including Deadline Detector's Google Calendar integration."
>
  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-max)] mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-4xl mb-4">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-fg-muted)] mb-10">Last updated: September 21, 2026</p>

    <div class="max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-fg-muted)] leading-relaxed space-y-6">
      <p>
        The tools listed on this site are small, independently-made desktop apps. None of them
        have a server — everything runs locally on your own machine. There is no analytics, no
        tracking, and nothing is ever sent to me or to any third party.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-[var(--color-fg)] mt-10 mb-3">
        Deadline Detector &amp; Google Calendar
      </h2>
      <p>
        Deadline Detector can optionally connect to your Google Calendar so your active deadlines
        show up alongside the rest of your schedule. This is entirely opt-in — the app works fully
        without it — and you can disconnect at any time from its menu.
      </p>
      <p>What this connection does:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>
          It requests the <code class="text-[var(--color-fg)]">calendar.events</code> scope only —
          permission to create, update, and delete events, not to read your existing calendar or
          any other Google data.
        </li>
        <li>
          For each active (not-yet-completed) deadline, it creates one event on your primary
          calendar with the task's name, due date/time, and its "next step" note if you set one.
        </li>
        <li>
          Editing a deadline's due date updates that event; completing or deleting a deadline
          removes it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Disconnecting removes every event the app created and revokes its access to your Google
          account — nothing is left behind.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The Google account credentials used for this (an encrypted refresh token) are stored only
        on your own computer, using your operating system's built-in encryption (Windows
        Credential/DPAPI). They never leave your machine except to talk directly to Google's
        Calendar API — I do not have a copy, and there is no server in between.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-2xl text-[var(--color-fg)] mt-10 mb-3">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about any of this? Reach me at
        <a href="mailto:manoatelier554@gmail.com" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">
          manoatelier554@gmail.com
        </a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
